If all the variables are ordinal, you can condense the raw data into a n-way contingency table.  As easier representation of this to work with might be to aggregate the data to the pattern of values across the variables, with an extra column for the count of cases with that pattern.

FYI, you can do this with the lavTables() function.  See the first help-page example:

HS9 <- HolzingerSwineford1939[,c("x1","x2","x3","x4","x5",
                                 "x6","x7","x8","x9")]
HSbinary <- as.data.frame( lapply(HS9, cut, 2, labels=FALSE) )

# counts & proportions for each response pattern
lavTables(HSbinary, dim = 0L, categorical = names(HSbinary))

