> Mal2<-solve(d, tol = 1.47364e-11)

> fit.cfaMod<-cfa(cfaMod,sample.cov=Mal2,sample.nobs=1193, estimator =
> "ML",std.lv <http://std.lv>=TRUE)

Why do you use Mal2 (which is solve(d)) as the input matrix? You should
use 'd'.

But when you call cor.smooth(), use a much more smaller tolerance, eg:

cor.smooth(Mal, eig.tol=10^-4)

Otherwise, lavaan may still flag your input matrix as non positive definite.

Yves.

